# Disable the vestigial built-in ethernet NIC inside my Dell monitor.
#
# tpx1 has its own copy of this rule (device-specifics/tpx1/) because it is the
# same monitor driven by a different machine. The rule body is identical; if you
# change one, change the other.
#
# The monitor has a USB hub used as a KVM, sharing keyboard/peripherals between
# my laptop dock and my desktop based on the selected input. On blackbox that hub
# hangs off the USB-C cable to the monitor, on the ASMedia ASM2142 (0000:04:00.0),
# and it presents as two trees: bus 3 (USB 2.0) carries everything I actually use
# - the Alesis codec, the condenser mic, the FiiO DAC and the HID - while bus 4
# (SuperSpeed) carries nothing but this NIC.
#
# The monitor's built-in RJ45 has never had a cable in it - my wired networking
# is the onboard Intel NIC (enp0s31f6). But the monitor's USB NIC is unstable: it
# re-enumerates repeatedly (on Windows it popped in and out of Device Manager over
# and over, with a connect sound each time) and the resulting bus churn constrains
# bandwidth for the audio devices sharing the hub's upstream cable.
#
# 2026-09-09: the whole controller was lost. The condenser mic on 3-1.3 failed to
# enumerate (device descriptor read/64, error -110) and forced a port power cycle
# while this NIC was enumerating on 4-1.1.3; the host controller then died outright
# ("xHCI host controller not responding, assume dead"), taking every device on the
# monitor hub down until the controller was reset. blackbox was at the time running
# a hand-written unbind rule that had never been committed to dotfiles and matched
# nothing (see below), so the NIC had never actually been disabled here.
#
# NOTE: the match is by USB *serial number*, NOT by port path or idVendor alone.
# This NIC is an RTL8153 (0bda:8153) - the SAME VID:PID as the laptop DOCK's
# ethernet, which must stay up. They can only be told apart by serial (or product):
#   monitor NIC: serial 001000001, product "USB 10/100/1000 LAN"   <- disable this
#   dock NIC   : serial 321601A34, product "Realtek LAN"           <- keep up
# Port path is NOT usable: the Thunderbolt dock re-assigns its USB devices to a
# different bus/path on every reconnect (seen jumping 2-1.1.1.3 -> 3-3.3.1.3),
# so a KERNEL==<path> match breaks on replug. Serial is topology-independent.
#
# We DEAUTHORIZE the device (ATTR{authorized}="0") rather than unbinding it.
# Reason: the RTL8153's usb_device binds to the "r8152-cfgselector" driver, not
# the generic "usb" driver, so the old `echo ... > drivers/usb/unbind` trick
# silently failed. authorized=0 is driver-independent and stronger - the device
# loads no driver and creates no netdev. It re-applies on every "add", so a
# re-enumeration storm just gets it deauthorized again each time.
#
# Two unbind variants have now failed and been replaced; don't reach for a third:
#   drivers/usb/unbind    - usb_device binds r8152-cfgselector, never matched.
#   DRIVER=="r8152" + ATTR{idVendor} - can't match either. On the usb_device the
#     driver is r8152-cfgselector; on the usb_interface (where the driver really
#     is r8152) ATTR{} reads only that interface's own sysfs dir and idVendor
#     lives on the parent, so the match silently fails. ATTRS{} walks parents,
#     ATTR{} does not.
#
# Verify it took effect with:
#   cat /sys/bus/usb/devices/4-1.1.3/authorized   # expect 0, and no interfaces
#
# If the monitor NIC is ever replaced, re-check its serial with:
#   for d in /sys/bus/usb/devices/*; do grep -q 8153 "$d/idProduct" 2>/dev/null \
#     && echo "$d $(cat $d/serial) $(cat $d/product)"; done
#
# To undo this change, remove the file from /etc/udev/rules.d/ and replug/reboot.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0bda", ATTR{idProduct}=="8153", ATTR{serial}=="001000001", ATTR{authorized}="0"
